# When writing release notes, follow these rules:

General rules for all release notes, regardless of `ChangeType`:
---------------

- For text in Title, there’s a limit of 160 characters (including spaces); in Description, there's a limit of 2,500 characters (including spaces).
- Title: Must be brief and effectively summarize the change so that readers get a good understanding of it without needing to open the description immediately. Where possible and relevant to the change, include the names of techical components, filenames, and APIs affected by the change.
- Description: Should provide necessary context, details, purpose of the change, and its immediate impact. Avoid excessive verbosity, internal jargon, or information already explicitly stated in the title.
- Apply consistent Markdown formatting:
    - Use sentence case in a title.
    - Don't put a period (.) at the end of a title.
    - **IMPORTANT - Title formatting based on description presence**:
        - If the release note has NO description, keep the title as regular text (not bold).
        - If the release note HAS a description, adjust the title to be bold.
        - Apply bold text (**text**) for UI elements within descriptions.
    - Apply bold text (**text**) for UI elements within descriptions only.
    - Bullet points (* item or - item): For lists of changes, benefits, or multiple affected components.
    - Use single backticks (`code`) for short code snippets, variable names, function names, specific file paths, or technical terms. For example, `journeyFactory`, `withDefaults`
    - Use triple backticks (```code```) for multi-line code examples, configuration snippets, or detailed syntax. Always specify the language for syntax highlighting if possible.
    - Links ([Link Text](URL)): To relevant documentation, migration guides, or API references. Recommended: For more information, see [Link Text](URL).
    - Use full semantic or calendar versioning with the v prefix: For example: Service SDK v19.0.0; Digital Engage v2025.04, Digital Lending v2025.03-LTS
- Use the terms in .vale/styles/config/vocabularies/Backbase/accept.txt, and avoid the terms in .vale/styles/config/vocabularies/Backbase/reject.txt.
- IMPORTANT: Write for the target audience: Developers, Business Analysts, Technical Managers, Product Owners. Do not ignore technical specifics and details such as component names, configuration options, data points, etc.

ChangeType-specific rules:
---------------

- FEATURE (New capabilities or Improved functionalities):

    - For Title, summarize the change so readers can understand it without needing to open the description.
        - Start the title with a past-tense verb: Added...; Improved...; Enabled...
        - If applicable, add the RFF number: RFF-<nr>: <Title>

    - For Description:
        - Can be skipped if the feature is effectively summarized by the title.
        - **CRITICAL**: If a description is provided, it MUST include business value, purpose, or benefits. A description without clear business value/purpose/benefits is incomplete and should be flagged for improvement.
        - Briefly describe the purpose of the change or the benefits it brings. Use the present tense and, if possible, adhere to the following format:
            - The <new-feature> lets you...;
            - The <journey-name> journey now lets bank admins…;
            - The <service-name> service now supports… 
        - Always explain WHY this feature matters to users - what problem it solves, what capability it enables, or what value it provides.
        - Provide links to the relevant documentation if available. Recommended: For more information, see [Link Text](URL).

- FIXED (Bug fixes)

    - For Title, describe *what* was fixed rather than *how* it was fixed. If possible, add just enough detail in the title so that a description isn’t required.
        - Start the title with a past-tense verb: Fixed... It is preferable to use "Fixed...", but you can use an alternate past-tense verb if it makes more sense in the provided context: Changed...; Reordered...
        - If applicable, add the MAINT number: MAINT-<nr>: <Title>
        - Do not add internal Jira ticket numbers.

    - For Description:
        - Can be skipped if the fix is effectively summarized by the title.
        - Describe any required changes to configuration or behavior as a result of the fix.

- DEPRECATED (Removal or replacement in a future breaking-change release)

    - For Title, use the following format: Removed in v<<Semver or CalVer that the item/functionality is going to be removed from>>: <<name of component/feature that's been deprecated, or description of default behavior that's changing>>

    - For Description, use:
  
            **Removed**
            <<name of old component/feature, or old behavior>>

            **Replaced by**
            <<Name of new component(s). If no replacement, state: No replacement.>>

    - If the thing being deprecated is a list of items rather than an individual item, summarize the list in Title and provide the list in full in Description.
    - If available, include links to detailed migration guides or upgrade instructions: For upgrade instructions, see [Link Text](URL).

- REMOVED (Removal of a previously deprecated item):

    - For Title, use the following format: Removed in v<<Semver or CalVer that the item/functionality is going to be removed from>>: <<name of component/feature that's been deprecated, or description of default behavior that's changing>>

    - For Description, use:
  
            **Removed**
            <<name of old component/feature, or old behavior>>

            **Replaced by**
            <<Name of new component(s). If no replacement, state: No replacement.>>

    - If the thing being removed is a list of items rather than an individual item, summarize the list in Title and provide the list in full in Description.
    - If available, include links to detailed migration guides or upgrade instructions: For upgrade instructions, see [Link Text](URL).

- DEPENDENCY (Upgrades to dependencies or external libraries):

    - For Title, use the following format: <Dependency Name> updated to v<x.x.x>

    - For Description: If available, add a link to the release notes of the dependency version: For more information, see the [<dependency-name> release notes](https://backbase.io/developers/documentation/release-notes/xxx).

- SECURITY (CVE or internally discovered security issues):

    - For Title, use one the following formats:
        - If making an internal change to fix a CVE: CVE-<nr>: Addressed <CVE-title> 
        For example, CVE-2023-5072: Addressed DoS Vulnerability in JSON-Java 
        - If updating a dependency to fix a CVE: CVE-<nr>: <Dependency Name> updated to v<x.x.x>
        For example, CVE-2023-5072: Angular updated to v17
        - If making an internal change to address a security issue: Made internal security improvements in <service or component name>.
        For example, Made internal security improvements in appointments-service

    - For Description, use: For more information, see [CVE-<nr>](https://www.cve.org/xxx).
        - If documenting an internal change to address a security issue, leave Description blank.